      "writtenOn": "2026-10-28T10:03:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\"Redfish-first\" is a convention with zero automated enforcement in the proposed CI\n\nThis design does not add codegen or vue-tsc to CI.\n\nand redfish-first-webui-architecture.md:147-155\n\nThe document\u0027s central promise — that the compiler catches remodeling and the wrong enum (\"off\" vs \"Off\") — is not true under the CI this design ratifies. I confirmed in eslint.config.js:150-184 that typescript-eslint rules are scoped to **/*.ts/**/*.tsx only; .vue files receive Vue-recommended + Prettier and no type-aware linting. npm run build is Vite (no type-check), and the design explicitly declines to add vue-tsc. So nothing — not lint, not build, not tests — fails when a developer reintroduces a camelCase DTO or binds chassis.PowerState. Every rule in this document is enforced solely by human reviewers remembering it.\n\nImpact:\nThe design ships as aspiration. Within one or two release cycles, under reviewer turnover and time pressure, remappers return and the document becomes a dead letter — exactly the fate it predicts for codegen (\"Generated types still get remodeled unless views bind Redfish names\").\n\nRecommendation:\nEither (a) add a CI gate now — a vue-tsc --noEmit type-check job, even against the hand-rolled types — or (b) reframe the document honestly as a review convention with no automated backing, and specify the review checklist maintainers must apply. A design that calls itself \"the contract those follow-on tools must keep\" must state who/what enforces the contract today. As written it is unenforceable.",
